# reading 2 of republic-function.md by claude-opus-5-5
# The "necessarily" of S10/S11 is not written modally: the argument is quantified over souls and men.
# S10 is read as a premise (good soul rules well) together with S8-S9 (without excellence, ends not fulfilled).
# S4: life is one of the soul's ends, so fulfilling the soul's ends well is read as living well, and failing them as living ill.
# S20: "happiness and not misery is profitable" is read in both directions: happy -> profitable, and not happy -> not profitable.
# S22: "injustice can never be more profitable than justice" is read as: the just are profitable and the unjust are not.
atom	excellent(x)	x has the excellence proper to the soul
atom	fulfils(x)	x fulfils the soul's own ends well (rules, superintends and deliberates well)
atom	lives_well(x)	x lives well
atom	lives_well_2(x)	faring well, prospering, so that one is blessed and happy
atom	just(x)	x is just
atom	happy(x)	x is blessed and happy
atom	profitable(x)	x's condition is profitable
P1	S9	∀x (¬excellent(x) → ¬fulfils(x))
P2	S10	∀x (excellent(x) → fulfils(x))
P3	S4	∀x ((fulfils(x) → lives_well(x)) ∧ (¬fulfils(x) → ¬lives_well(x)))
P4	S12	∀x (just(x) ↔ excellent(x))
C1	S14	∀x ((just(x) → lives_well(x)) ∧ (¬just(x) → ¬lives_well(x)))	from P1 P2 P3 P4
P5	S16	∀x ((lives_well_2(x) → happy(x)) ∧ (¬lives_well_2(x) → ¬happy(x)))
C2	S18	∀x ((just(x) → happy(x)) ∧ (¬just(x) → ¬happy(x)))	from C1 P5
P6	S20	∀x ((happy(x) → profitable(x)) ∧ (¬happy(x) → ¬profitable(x)))
C	S22	∀x ((just(x) → profitable(x)) ∧ (¬just(x) → ¬profitable(x)))	from C2 P6
